5:[["$","$L10","5.7",{"topic":{"id":"5.7","title":"Emerging Cyber Security Threats","minutes":12,"summary":"Evaluate new threats through plausible scenarios, dependencies, and controls rather than hype or a list of alarming headlines.","sections":[{"title":"The changing threat landscape","body":["Ransomware, credential theft, supply-chain compromise, cloud misconfiguration, API abuse, and social engineering remain interconnected risks. AI can lower the cost of persuasive deception and assist vulnerability discovery, while also improving defensive analysis. A threat becomes relevant when a plausible actor, capability, exposure, and impact align with the organization’s context.","Deepfake voice or video can reinforce impersonation and payment fraud. A convincing call should not replace an independently verified approval process. Quantum computing raises long-term cryptographic considerations, but claims that all current encryption has already been broken are inaccurate. Distinguish present incidents, emerging capabilities, and speculative futures."]},{"title":"Scenario-based risk assessment","body":["Choose a critical business process and map its identities, devices, providers, data, and recovery dependencies. Describe a realistic attack path and the controls that could prevent, detect, or limit it. Consider ordinary failures as well as deliberate attacks. A supplier outage and a ransomware incident may require similar continuity capabilities.","Use dated sources such as ENISA threat reports and relevant national advisories. A report’s ranking is not a universal probability for every organization. Record likelihood assumptions, impact, uncertainty, and the evidence behind them. Avoid copying a global trend into a local risk register without checking relevance."]},{"title":"Resilience and responsible innovation","body":["Layer identity protection, patching, segmentation, backups, monitoring, and exercised response. Verify that backups are isolated, restorable, and compatible with business recovery needs. Test human approval processes for payments and privileged changes. Technology alone cannot fix a process that accepts urgency as authorization.","For AI and other new tools, define acceptable use, data handling, evaluation, human oversight, and incident escalation. Preserve provenance where feasible and avoid treating a detector’s result as conclusive. The most useful future-facing analysis identifies measurable preparations rather than predicting a single inevitable attack."]}],"practice":{"title":"Build a deepfake payment scenario","scenario":"A fictional manager receives a realistic voice call requesting an urgent supplier payment.","steps":["Map the approval and payment dependencies.","Identify a control that does not depend on recognizing the voice.","Define evidence to preserve if the request is fraudulent.","Write a recovery and escalation plan with clear owners."],"debrief":"Out-of-band verification and dual approval can remain effective even when the deception is technically convincing."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["it-2021","dpdp"],"sources":["enisa","deepfake","it2026","nist-csf"],"labId":"ai","unit":5,"objectives":["Explain the changing threat landscape and apply it to an investigation.","Explain scenario-based risk assessment and apply it to an investigation.","Explain resilience and responsible innovation and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-5.7","prompt":"Which control best addresses a convincing voice-clone payment request?","options":["Trusting the voice if it sounds familiar.","Independent call-back and documented payment approval.","Paying faster to avoid embarrassment.","Relying only on an AI detector."],"answer":1,"explanation":"A process-based verification control does not depend solely on the perceived authenticity of a voice.","unit":5}}}]]
